steps.body:
Recursion uses Node's call stack, which overflows after roughly 10,000 frames, and often fewer for real functions. One long snake-shaped region in a 200 by 200 grid is enough to crash it with `RangeError: Maximum call stack size exceeded`. The fix is to keep your own stack in an array.

```javascript harness
const visited = [
  [false, false],
  [false, false],
];
const startR = 0;
const startC = 0;
{{code}}
console.log(JSON.stringify(visited));
```

```javascript
const stack = [[startR, startC]];
visited[startR][startC] = true;
while (stack.length > 0) {
  const [r, c] = stack.pop(); // take the most recently pushed cell
  // check neighbors, mark them visited, push them
}
```

`push` adds to the end and `pop` takes from the end, so the newest cell is explored first. That is what makes it depth-first. An array can hold millions of cells, so this version works on any grid that fits in memory.

The neighbor logic matches recursive DFS: try the four direction offsets, check the bounds, then skip cells that are visited or not part of the region. The one change is **when** you mark a cell. Mark it visited as you push it. If you waited until you pop it, the same cell could be pushed several times by different neighbors and get counted twice.

example.code:
const input = require("fs").readFileSync(0, "utf8");
const grid = input.trim().split("\n").map((line) => line.trim().split(""));
const rows = grid.length;
const cols = grid[0].length;
const directions = [[1, 0], [-1, 0], [0, 1], [0, -1]];
const visited = Array.from({ length: rows }, () => new Array(cols).fill(false));

function islandSize(startR, startC) {
  const stack = [[startR, startC]];
  visited[startR][startC] = true;
  let size = 0;
  while (stack.length > 0) {
    const [r, c] = stack.pop();
    size++;
    for (const [dr, dc] of directions) {
      const nr = r + dr;
      const nc = c + dc;
      if (nr < 0 || nr >= rows || nc < 0 || nc >= cols) {
        continue;
      }
      if (!visited[nr][nc] && grid[nr][nc] === "#") {
        visited[nr][nc] = true;
        stack.push([nr, nc]);
      }
    }
  }
  return size;
}

const sizes = [];
for (let r = 0; r < rows; r++) {
  for (let c = 0; c < cols; c++) {
    if (grid[r][c] === "#" && !visited[r][c]) {
      sizes.push(islandSize(r, c));
    }
  }
}
console.log(`Islands: ${sizes.length}`);
console.log(`Sizes: ${sizes.join(", ")}`);

