steps.body:
Backtracking is DFS over *choices* instead of cells. Keep the current partial answer in a `path` array and follow three moves:

```javascript harness
const nums = [1, 2, 3];
const path = [];
const results = [];

function explore(start, sum) {
  results.push([...path]);
  for (let i = start; i < nums.length; i++) {
    {{code}}
  }
}

explore(0, 0);
console.log(results.map((subset) => `{${subset.join(", ")}}`).join(" "));
```

```javascript
path.push(nums[i]);           // choose
explore(i + 1, sum + nums[i]); // explore everything that starts with this choice
path.pop();                    // un-choose, so the next loop turn starts clean
```

The loop inside `explore(start, sum)` runs `i` from `start`, never from 0. Each later call only looks to the right, so `{1, 2}` is built once and `{2, 1}` never appears. It also fixes the output order: everything that starts with 1 comes before anything that starts with 2.

`path` is one shared array that keeps changing. To save a solution, store a copy with `results.push([...path])`. Pushing `path` itself saves the same array every time, and it is empty by the end. Printing `path.join(", ")` right away also works.

**Pruning** skips work that cannot succeed. When the values are in increasing order and `sum + nums[i]` is already too big, every later value is too big as well, so `break` out of the loop.

Permutations use the same pattern with a `used` boolean array instead of a start index.

example.code:
const input = require("fs").readFileSync(0, "utf8");
const [limitLine, numsLine] = input.trim().split("\n");
const limit = Number(limitLine);
const nums = numsLine.trim().split(/\s+/).map(Number);
const path = [];
const results = [];

function explore(start, sum) {
  results.push([...path]);
  for (let i = start; i < nums.length; i++) {
    if (sum + nums[i] > limit) {
      break;
    }
    path.push(nums[i]);
    explore(i + 1, sum + nums[i]);
    path.pop();
  }
}

explore(0, 0);
for (const subset of results) {
  console.log(`{${subset.join(", ")}}`);
}
console.log(`Total: ${results.length}`);

